<DocsLayout title="Aurora Technical Reference" description="Definitions of public project status labels and evidence terminology." heading="Technical reference" eyebrow="DOCUMENTATION / REFERENCE">
<p class="docs-kicker">STATUS VOCABULARY</p><h2>Use status labels precisely.</h2><dl class="reference-definitions"><div><dt>PLANNED</dt><dd>Approved future work; no implementation is implied.</dd></div><div><dt>DESIGNED</dt><dd>A specification or architecture decision exists; implementation is not implied.</dd></div><div><dt>IMPLEMENTED</dt><dd>An identifiable implementation exists in the relevant repository.</dd></div><div><dt>TESTED</dt><dd>Relevant tests were executed and their results are recorded.</dd></div><div><dt>VERIFIED</dt><dd>Explicit verification criteria have been satisfied with inspectable evidence.</dd></div><div><dt>PROTOTYPE</dt><dd>An experimental or incomplete implementation exists; production readiness is not implied.</dd></div><div><dt>BLOCKED</dt><dd>Progress cannot continue because a concrete dependency or constraint is unresolved.</dd></div><div><dt>NOT VERIFIED</dt><dd>Available evidence is insufficient to make a stronger claim.</dd></div></dl><h2>Evidence hierarchy</h2><p>Prefer source references, test logs, build artifacts, reproducible procedures and dated reports. A source file alone does not prove runtime behavior. A passing unit test does not prove integration or security.</p>
</DocsLayout>